---
name: dev-fix
version: 1.4.0
description: lead dev-fix assignment for a dev bead reopened after a failed sanity check. Same bead (task_id), same branch; its open child finding beads are the work.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - pr_target
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
optional_variables:
  - sanity_bead
defaults:
  sanity_bead: ""
metadata:
  type: dev-fix
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" mode="dev-fix">
  <description><![CDATA[{% if sanity_bead %}Sanity check `{{ sanity_bead | string | cdata_escape }}` failed dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` and reopened it.{% else %}The task assigner reopened dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` after plan review.{% endif %} Its open child finding beads are the work. `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is still the assignment.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[`{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is not linked. Commit the fixes there; its PR lands on the current top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}`. Never edit another branch and never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` in the worktree (a conflict is part of the fix), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Each check, its command and its refusal code: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) difficulty: your `atm members --json` model is in `DIFFICULTY_MODELS[metadata.difficulty]`, else `DIFFICULTY_MISMATCH`; (6) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`. `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. Reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. On success claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it and do not start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee; still `closed` means it was never reopened), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (its open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each. Report it to the task assigner: the bead, why it is not ready, and which bead or agent has to move. Then wait.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` and its open child beads (`bd list --parent {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --json`). Read the complete plan and enumerate all tasks that must be completed in the primary and any child beads; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time; close each child when it is fixed: `bd close <child> --reason "<short sha>: <what fixed it>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`. Then go through the checklist again one item at a time and confirm each is done, and re-check the whole bead: the sanity check reports what it saw, not everything that is missing. Do not weaken a test or criterion to make a finding go away. The bead's `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` still govern the fix: re-read each one it names.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`, find the top with `/sc-gh-stack-view --json` (`gh_stack_view.py --json`): in `stacks[]` take the stack whose `rows[]` contain the branch `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` (or whose `trunk` is it) and the `branch` of its last open row other than `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`, or the trunk itself when the stack has no open layer; then `git rebase origin/<top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Then open the PR against that top, or confirm the open one: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` (never `--draft`), run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Close the bead and the task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "dev complete at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/dev-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/dev-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. In the vars, `rebased_onto`, `pr_number`, `pr_url` and `stack_view` come from step d, and `deliverables_md` lists each finding and what fixed it. The sanity check then runs again on the new head. Only this close frees your queue.]]></step>
    <step id="e1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. Declare failure with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "DEV_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
